                <pb facs="0525" n="413" sameAs="frus:frus1907p1/pg413"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 3294/7.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to Ambassador
                        McCormick</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1907-01-22"><hi rendition="#italic">January 22,
                        1907</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Bacon informs Mr. McCormick from facts before the department at present that
                    it appears that Mr. Jacobs is an American citizen, and as such is entitled to
                    proper protection in the premises. Procedure in this case is contrary in several
                    important particulars to that set forth in Minister McLane’s dispatch No. 74 of
                    January 14, 1889. Mr. Bacon also instructs Mr. McCormick, if present proceedings
                    are based solely upon comity, to state to the minister of foreign affairs that
                    the United States seems to have as strong a claim to consideration of the French
                    Government in asking adequate protection for an American citizen as the
                    Argentine Government has to obtain his surrender.)</p>
            </div>
